  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-shell-progress",
      "text": "English | [中文](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/blob/main/README.zh.md)"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-shell-progress",
      "text": "<!-- Badges. `ci.yml` and `harness-matrix.yml` both run on `main`, so their badges\n     track it. `release.yml` is TAG-triggered and has no status on a branch — a\n     branch badge for it renders \"no status\" — so its badge is pinned to a tag and\n     `?branch=v0.9.1` below must be bumped with each release. -->\n[](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-shell-progress)\n[](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/blob/main/LICENSE)\n[](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/blob/main/package.json)\n[](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/actions/workflows/harness-matrix.yml)\n[](https://github.com/9087/dsh-shell-progress/actions/workflows/release.yml)"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-shell-progress",
      "text": "A DeepSeek Harness plugin that shows **where a multi-step shell command is right\nnow**, as a segmented progress bar inside the tool-call card.\n\n<!-- Absolute, not `docs/assets/running.png`: npmjs.com renders only the README it\n     snapshotted at publish time and does not serve the repository tree, so a relative\n     path shows a broken image there. The file still lives in the repo, and\n     `pnpm run e2e` rewrites it, so this URL follows the code. -->"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-shell-progress",
      "text": "*The real card, expanded, with `3/4` done and step 4 in flight.*"
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "Collapsed, the card's summary row carries a segmented bar, one segment per top-level step (pending, running or done), beside a `3/4` counter, so where the command is and how many steps are left read at a glance. Hover a segment and it opens that step's card, showing its command line, its output and its time."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "In the hover card, each step gets a one-line comment above its command line, spelling out what that line is for. (The lines come from the model, written alongside the command so a run never waits for them; each distinct command is asked for once, and a card whose notes missed the record fetches them the first time you hover or open it. They can be switched off on the DSH Settings page under \"Shell Progress\" and are on by default.)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "Expanding the card shows the command's steps, one row each: the line's command, plus its time and output; the step in flight carries a running clock."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "A command the model runs in the background (`run_in_background: true`) is a job, and its card says so: the title reads \"run a background job\", with the warning mark beside it explaining on hover, and no bar or counter — the record is written the moment the job starts, so a bar could only claim a position it never saw. Expanding the card lists the command's statements; the output stays with the shell, in the **Background jobs** list at the top of the conversation, which also stops the job. The command runs unmodified (so its output is byte-exact), and a long build or test suite no longer holds the turn while it runs."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "A command that cannot be split, or that the plugin declines to track, is still ONE command: the card draws one segment, and a `⚠` after the title explains on hover that the command was not split, so the bar describes the whole command rather than per-step progress. The command itself runs exactly as it does under the `pwsh` tool, and its output obeys the same per-step budget."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "In DSH, open the **Plugins** page and click **Add plugin**, then enter `dsh-shell-progress` (the npm package name).\n\nOr from the command line:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-shell-progress\n```\n\nOr mount it by hand in the profile's `cordis.patch.yml`:\n\n```yaml\n- insert:\n    - id: shell-progress\n      name: dsh-shell-progress\n```\n\nEither way, restart `dsh web` afterwards (the host loads plugins once, at start-up)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "```yaml\n- insert:\n    - id: shell-progress\n      name: dsh-shell-progress\n      config:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "| Field | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `enabled` | `true` | Register the `shell_progress` tool. |\n| `pollIntervalMs` | `120` | How often the host reads the running process's output. |\n| `maxStepOutputChars` | `8192` | Per-step output kept in the persisted progress. |\n| `maxSteps` | `400` | A command split into more steps than this runs unmodified. |\n| `liveRetentionMs` | `300000` | How long a settled call's live record is kept for a late poll. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "When a step's output passes `maxStepOutputChars`, the lines that do not fit are dropped from\nthe CARD's copy, and that step's output is followed by the number of characters that are\nmissing (in the step list and in the segment's hover card alike). Without it a truncated\nstep reads exactly like a quiet one. The budget is the card's memory, not the tool result:\nthe model-visible stdout comes from the shell seam and is unaffected by it (the seam's own\n`truncated` / `spillPath` reporting applies as it always does)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "Development, testing and publishing details live in [DEVELOP.md](DEVELOP.md) and [PUBLISH.md](PUBLISH.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT"
    }
  ],
